        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br> 
The Role of Visual Cues in Spatial Orientation experiment was one of three sensory motor and performance experiments performed during the 1998 Neurolab mission.  This experiment focused on how the balance between visual and vestibular cues shifts toward the visual system in microgravity. 
<P> 
Using virtual reality, this investigation was designed to study how visual scene content and symmetry influenced the astronauts perception of up and down, to determine how quickly a moving visual scene would produce the illusion of self-motion,  and to explore how the direction of perceived "down" altered the ability to recognize shapes and interpret curvature from shading. 
<P>
 Information gained from this experiment will help investigators understand why, in the absence of gravity, astronauts become dependent on visual stimuli.  This information is also of potential value to medical researchers seeking insight into inner ear impairments and other balance disorders in Earth-bound patients, as well as related rehabilitative testing and training methods for those individuals.  As an added value, portable head-mounted displays similar to the one developed for use in this experiment may prove useful for patients, perhaps someday even providing visual prostheses for the vestibularly impaired. 
 <P> 
APPROACH:<br>  
The Role of Visual Cues in Spatial Orientation experiment was performed using NASA's Virtual Environment Generator (VEG), a head-mounted display, which allowed the subjects to view a succession of visual scenes rendered by the VEG graphic computer.  The VEG also tracked the motion of the head, so scenes that were displayed appeared stable when the head moved.   The astronauts participated in three types of spatial orientation experiments:<br>
1)	the Virtual Tilting Room Test (ROOM)<BR> 2)  the Linear Vection Test (VECTION)<br> 3)  the Object Recognition Test (OBJECT).  Tests were performed with the astronaut free-floating and, alternately, wearing a constant force spring harness that provided artificial "down" cues to the shoulders and hips. Preflight ROOM, OBJECT and VECTION tests were performed 1 year before launch in NASA's training airplane, the KC-135, and in the laboratory at 90, 89, 60 and 59 days before launch.  The OBJECT test was performed on flight day 3, and the ROOM and VECTION tests were performed on flight day 4, with an additional session performed by one subject on flight day 16.  Postflight, all three tests were performed on the day of landing and 1, 2, 4 and 6 days after landing. 
<P> 
The ROOM test allowed investigators to study the influence of visual scene symmetry, scene rotation, orientation expectation, and tactile/proprioceptive cues on perceived self-tilt.  Subjects wearing the VEG head mounted display viewed the interior of a virtual room.  Two presentation types were used, static and continuously rotating at constant angular velocity.  The VEG joystick, also called the Subject Interface Device (SID), recorded subject responses.  The subject made comments using a microphone and entered notes after testing using the IBM Thinkpad laptop computer.   
<P> 
During both the ROOM and VECTION tests, subjects wore the Subject Restraint System for testing under free-floating and simulated "standing upright" conditions.  When free-floating, the subject's body was not in contact with the Spacelab; loose cloth tethers clipped between the restraint harness, D-rings on the center aisle, and rack handrails prevented the subject from drifting.  When the subject was "standing," constraint force springs connecting the harness to the floor allowed the subject to stand erect with his feet on the floor, with proprioceptive and tactile compressive loading on the trunk and lower extremities.   
<P> 
Several hypotheses were tested during the ROOM sessions, including:<BR> 1) Visual reorientation illusions could be created in the frontal (roll) plane using a VEG-controlled, visual stimulus.  Inversion illusions could be elicited in subjects susceptible to them, because the visual cues for static self-tilt perception are usually derived from the ambiguous underlying horizontal and vertical visual symmetries in conventional room interiors<BR> 2) A subject's indication of the subjective vertical can be predicted in rooms with differing degrees of visual symmetry (dotted, walled, or furnished rooms).  A subject's self-tilt report in static and rotating virtual rooms depends (in a predictable way) not only on scene symmetry, but on expectation, which is determined by internal sensory cues associated with 0-g fluid shift and saccular unloading.  The latter will be manipulated using preconditioning visual and haptic stimuli<BR> 3) As subjects adapt to weightlessness, their self-orientation becomes less biased by internal sensory cues and increasingly veridical.<P>  The VECTION test was used with and without haptic cues to determine how quickly a moving visual scene would produce the illusion of self-motion.  Haptic cues are clues that allow orientation by providing input from the restraint system that, for example, forces the feet against the floor, thereby giving the feeling of standing.  The VEG was programmed to present a binocular "looming vection" display to the subject in successive 30-second tests.  The stimulus velocity and subject orientation were manipulated across tests.  Objects in the scene moved radially on the display, simulating subject movement along a path parallel to the visual axis.   
<P> 
Previous research on the role of visual cues in 0-g spatial orientation has addressed the phenomenon of rotational vection about the roll axis of the head (roll circular vection).  The results suggested an increasing dependence on visual and haptic cues and indicated that less weight was given to otolithic cues (probably because their response to head tilt seems initially unfamiliar in 0-g).  Otolith organs physically responded equally well to linear acceleration and head tilt.  Therefore, it is important to know whether otolith responses to linear acceleration were also given less weight.  If so, the linear, as well as angular, component of visually induced self-motion should show changes in 0-g.  If all otolith cues are discounted in 0-g, the following was hypothesized for the VECTION test:  1)  the latency of linear vection onset to constant velocity visual stimulation would be shorter; and 2)  vection magnitude would increase faster and with fewer dropouts than on Earth.
<P> 
 The OBJECT test used two types of stimuli to study the influence of perceived "down" on the visual recognition of complex figures and interpretation of shading gradients.  Whether an item was determined to be concave or convex depended on how the visual system interpreted the shading.  Hypotheses for the OBJECT test included:<br> 1) For complex figure recognition and shading gradient interpretation, the critical factor would be subjective orientation of the "perceptual down," the exocentric frame of reference cognitively adopted by the subject, not gravity<br> 2) Because subjects can voluntarily change their exocentric reference frame, the experiment should show that this change results in object recognition and concavity/convexity interpretation.
 <P>
 RESULTS:<br>  
Eight of 8 nominally scheduled sessions were performed early in flight.  One additional session was performed on flight day 16 before a hardware failure that prevented further additional sessions.  Data appear to be of good quality based on the initial evaluation. Results from this experiment are expected to add insight into how the nervous system establishes a new balance between information gathered from the eyes, inner ear, and joints.  This could help in the design of the next generation spacecraft, in the testing of patients with neurological diseases, and in understanding the relative role of vision in human spatial orientation not only in space, but also in our lives on Earth. <P>
        </div>

